   "intro": "A rest day. A psalm about trusting God while other people seem to get ahead.",
   "keyVerse": "Rest in the LORD, and wait patiently for him.",
   "reflection": [
    "“Don’t fret.” David keeps coming back to it in this psalm. Fretting is that restless, circling worry, often about other people: who has more, who gets away with things, who seems further ahead.",
    "Instead: “Trust in the LORD, and do good.” “Commit your way to the LORD.” “Rest in the LORD, and wait patiently for him.” Trust isn’t only a feeling. It’s also doing the next good thing, and leaving the rest with God.",
    "“Delight yourself in the LORD, and he will give you the desires of your heart” isn’t a trick for getting what you want. As people delight in God, their desires slowly change too. This week: stars, a burning bush, eagles, your name, a straight path, a hand reaching out. Which picture of trust stays with you?"
   ],
   "sitWith": "Let your body rest, shoulders down, hands open. Pray: I rest in you, Lord. I will wait.",
   "prayer": "Lord, I fret about many things. Help me trust you and do good, commit my way to you, and rest. I wait for you, patiently or not, but I wait. Amen.",
   "practice": "Look back at this week’s journal. Write one sentence that starts: “This week I learned to trust God with…”",
   "journal": "Which picture of trust from this week will you carry with you?",
